{"text": "Mateo learns most successfully through direct experience before abstract explanation; tactile diagrams and real objects; extra time to scan and explore; and consistent routines with preview of changes. These strategies make instruction clearer and reduce the amount of energy spent simply gaining access.\n\nThe profile also identifies predictable barriers. Mateo may struggle with incidental learning from distant events; fast group discussion; unannounced room changes; and materials that are only visual or auditory. We should not treat these points as a list of personal deficits. Each one directs the team toward a response. For example, a barrier caused by pace may require additional processing time; an environmental barrier may require a quieter, safer, or more accessible setting; and task complexity may require modeling, chunking, direct instruction, or an alternate response.\n\nThe goal is not to remove every productive challenge. The goal is to remove barriers that prevent the student from engaging with the intended learning. Presenter 3 will now connect the profile’s sensory, medical, behavioural, and safety information to the EA’s day-to-day responsibilities.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-B-04.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Mateo", "start": 0.125, "end": 0.65, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 5}, {"text": "learns", "start": 0.6625, "end": 1.05, "start_char": 6, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "most", "start": 1.0625, "end": 1.325, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 17}, {"text": "successfully", "start": 1.3375, "end": 2.0125, "start_char": 18, "end_char": 30}, {"text": "through", "start": 2.025, "end": 2.25, "start_char": 31, "end_char": 38}, {"text": "direct", "start": 2.2625, "end": 2.65, "start_char": 39, "end_char": 45}, {"text": "experience", "start": 2.6625, "end": 3.325, "start_char": 46, "end_char": 56}, {"text": "before", "start": 3.3375, "end": 3.675, "start_char": 57, "end_char": 63}, {"text": "abstract", "start": 3.6875, "end": 4.